## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical Azure ML routing, per-tenant isolation, and real-world deployment controls for AI services.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant-aware routing and dynamic model versioning
- Canary rollouts, rollback safety, and traffic splitting
- Data isolation via per-tenant storage and Key Vault
- Auditing with Microsoft Purview and synthetic tests
- Latency optimization through regional endpoint caching

## Code Example
```javascript
// pseudo
async function infer(req, res) {
  const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant-id'];
  const version = await getTenantVersion(tenant);
  const endpoint = `/ml/${tenant}/models/${version}/score`;
  const result = await mlClient.invoke(endpoint, req.body);
  res.json(result);
}
```

## Architecture Considerations
- **Latency Goals**: Target P99 < 200ms for inference; use Azure Front Door with traffic routing closest to tenant data residency
- **Canary Rollouts**: Use Azure App Configuration feature flags with percentage-based rollout; monitor error rates before full migration
- **Cost Controls**: Implement per-tenant quota via Azure Policy, enable auto-scaling on ML endpoints, and use Azure Cost Management alerts
- **Testing**: Load test with tenant isolation validation; use Azure DevOps pipelines for tenant-specific smoke tests
- **Auditing**: Enable Azure Monitor logs with tenant correlation; use Microsoft Purview for data lineage and compliance reporting
